# 10/17/26 The furification continues
It's been about a month since I went to [furry migration](/blog/posts/9.14.26_fm_'26.html), and since then my furification has only grown stronger. My new friend Ninten has been helping me create my own fursona! It's going to be a mix of species, about 80% crow, 10% dog, and 10% robot.

Last week I went to a furry picnic with her and she started designing my fursona with me. I put together a mood board to help inform the art direction a bit:
![A mood board with many pictures, including a crow, uzi doorman, cassette tapes, Luvstarkei, another person's fursuit, and screenshots from the videogame Ghostrunner](/resources/blog/fursona_mood_board.png)

What she created looks really great so far, and she's working on the final coloring of it right now!! I'm really excited to be able to show it off, I'll definitely post it here once it's done. One of the most embarrassing things about telling someone I'm a furry is all the followup questions, "can I see a picture of your fursuit" "no I don't have one yet", "well what's your fursona" "uhh I'm still working on it but it's a crow and a dog and a robot all in one". I'd never call someone else a fake furry, but the self-critical part of my brain makes me feel like a faker when I answer those questions 😭.

ALSO, I'm working on learning how to sew right now so that I can make myself a fursuit. So far I've just done a couple of beginner projects and haven't used a sewing machine yet. I made a little sock for my cat (which stayed on for about half a second before she kicked it off), and I made duck feet shoe covers!
![small tube of fabric the right size to fit on a cat foot](/resources/blog/10.17.26%20cat%20sock.jpg)
![the "cat sock" on my cat](/resources/blog/10.17.26%20cat%20sock%20on%20cat.jpg)

![orange shoe covers shaped like duck feet worn on feet](/resources/blog/10.17.26%20shoe%20cover.jpg)
![orange shoe covers shaped like duck feet upside down on a table. There's an elastic band used to wrap around the wearer's shoe](/resources/blog/10.17.26%20shoe%20cover%20close.jpg)

I'm proud of how good the shape came out! I created the pattern myself in a photo editor. And on the inside I added some foam and pipe cleaners so that the important part would retain its shape while wearing it:
![The inside of the shoe covers. There's a foam piece sitting on the fabric along with 3 pipe cleaners taped onto it](/resources/blog/10.17.26%20shoe%20cover%20inside.jpg)

The next project I'm planning on working on is a pair of crow feet from a youtube tutorial I found. It'll be a lot of sewing, so I'm going to learn to use a sewing machine before attempting it. These duck feet where already a few hours of sewing, and they're quite basic in comparison.

To show my appreciation to my new friends being so welcoming (and for working so hard to make me the perfect fursona), I made them a few gifts that I'm proud of!

First for Ninten I made her this fursuit fan:
![A bone shaped fan. On the top there's an air vent that spells out the name "nintendoge"](/resources/blog/10.17.26%20fursuit%20fan.jpg)

The purpose of a fursuit fan is to cool you down while you have your head on. Recently it's become popular to have these fans shaped like popsicles, that way when you stick the fan in your fursuit's mouth it looks like you're putting a popsicle in your mouth. I thought it would be a neat idea to make one bone shaped so it looks like you're knawing on a bone when you cool yourself off. It's maybe not the most practical shape for such a fan, but it does work! I'd share pictures of the inside, but I forgot to take any :'(. I'm pretty happy that I've gotten to a point with soldering and electronics now where I can pick up basic pieces and be very confident about piecing them together into something working

And for her sister I made this set of pins:
![A set of 3 pins, one has a fursona, another has the logo for this website, and the third has link from legend of zelda](/resources/blog/10.17.26%20pins.jpg)

But these aren't just pins, they are working nfc tags! The top left directs to her instagram when scanned, the right goes to this website, and the bottom one is a working amiibo. There's a trick to making these, when pressing the pin together if you just put the nfc tag sticker directly on the metal backing it won't work at all. The metal backing interferes with the radio signal that comes off of the tag. You need a material that reflects the radio signal so that it doesn't reach the metal backing. I got a sheet of adhesive ferrite which I evenly apply to the metal backing, then I apply the nfc sticker on top of that, then I press the rest of the pin as normal. After doing that it'll work as a normal nfc tag! To my knowledge no one else has done this before with a pin specifically. With how many people are in the world it *probably* has been done, but I like to think the few I have and the 3 I gave to her are the only ones in existence of their kind.